# Snyk policy — exceptions to the security gate, each with a reason and a date.
#
# 🔑 An exception without an expiry is not a decision, it is a permanent blind spot. Every entry
# here carries `expires`, so the question re-opens by itself and somebody has to answer it again.
#
# ⛔ What this file is NOT for: raising `--severity-threshold` to silence a class of findings, or
# dropping `--dev` from the scan. Both hide the next advisory as well as this one. The scan stays
# as strict as it was and the exception is named, one advisory at a time.
#
# ⚠️ `snyk ignore` rewrites this file and would drop these comments. Edit it by hand.

version: v1.25.0

ignore:
  # ── SNYK-JS-URIJS-19963963 — Infinite loop in uri-js@4.4.1 ────────────────────────────────
  #
  # Found by the weekly run on 21 September 2026. The same job passed on 13, 14 and 15 September,
  # so the advisory is days old, not a finding that sat here ignored.
  #
  # The whole path, and it is the only route to this package in the tree:
  #
  #     eslint@10.9.0 > ajv@6.15.0 > uri-js@4.4.1
  #
  # 📌 Three facts decide this, and Snyk supplies the first one itself:
  #
  #   1. `Issues with no direct upgrade or patch` — there is no fixed version of uri-js. 4.4.1 is
  #      the last release; the package is effectively unmaintained.
  #   2. It is reachable only through **eslint**, a devDependency. This repository publishes
  #      nothing to npm — it is a test framework — so no consumer ever installs this tree.
  #   3. The real fix is not ours to make. `ajv@8` dropped uri-js for `fast-uri`; eslint pins
  #      `ajv@6`. This clears the day eslint moves, and not before.
  #
  # ⚠️ So the alternative to an exception is a gate that is red every week for something the
  # author cannot act on — and this repository already has the rule for that, in `5.2` of the
  # interview notes and in the CI job it produced: **a gate that cannot be acted on teaches people
  # to ignore it.** The gate stays honest only if the exception is written down, not if the
  # threshold moves.
  #
  # 📌 The key is the advisory ID, not the package. Any *other* vulnerability in uri-js — or a
  # second path arriving at it — still fails this job.
  #
  # ⏰ Expires 21 December 2026, three months, which is twelve more weekly runs. If eslint has not
  # moved by then the job goes red again and the decision gets made a second time, with whatever
  # is true in December rather than what was true in September.
  SNYK-JS-URIJS-19963963:
    - '*':
        reason: >-
          High-severity infinite loop in uri-js@4.4.1, reachable only as
          eslint@10.9.0 > ajv@6.15.0 > uri-js@4.4.1. Snyk reports no direct upgrade or patch:
          4.4.1 is the final release of an unmaintained package. The dependency is dev-only and
          this repository publishes nothing, so it reaches no consumer. The fix belongs to eslint,
          which pins ajv@6; ajv@8 replaced uri-js with fast-uri. Re-examined on expiry.
        expires: 2026-12-21T00:00:00.000Z
        created: 2026-09-21T00:00:00.000Z

  # ── SNYK-JS-ZOD-20510278 — Allocation of resources without limits in zod@4.4.3 ──────────────
  #
  # Found by the weekly run on 5 October 2026. The same job passed on 28 September, and nothing was
  # committed in between, so the advisory is new, not something that sat here unnoticed.
  #
  # 📌 Why an exception and not an upgrade:
  #
  #   1. Snyk reports `No upgrade or patch available`, and it said so on 5 October, three weeks
  #      after zod@4.6.5 was published — so the latest release is affected too. There is no
  #      version to move to.
  #   2. zod is a devDependency of a private package. This repository publishes nothing to npm,
  #      so no consumer ever installs it.
  #   3. What zod parses here is the response of the test target — the Conduit instances this
  #      suite runs against — not input from an attacker. Resource exhaustion on that path costs a
  #      slow test run, not a service.
  #
  # 📌 The other two findings of the same run were fixable and were fixed, not excepted:
  # brace-expansion went from 5.0.9 to 5.0.12 inside the range eslint > minimatch already allows.
  #
  # ⏰ Expires 7 January 2027, three months. If zod has shipped a fix by then, the job goes red and
  # the answer is an upgrade; if not, the decision is made again with what is true in January.
  SNYK-JS-ZOD-20510278:
    - '*':
        reason: >-
          High-severity allocation of resources without limits in zod@4.4.3. Snyk reports no
          upgrade or patch, including for the latest release. zod is a devDependency of a private
          package that publishes nothing, and it parses only the responses of the suite's own test
          target. Re-examined on expiry.
        expires: 2027-01-07T00:00:00.000Z
        created: 2026-10-07T00:00:00.000Z
